{"iter": 1, "reply": "# FILE: spend/__init__.py\n```python\n\"\"\"spend \u2014 a small but real personal-expense-tracker CLI.\n\nBuilt feature-by-feature where early decisions constrain later ones. The\nload-bearing decision is the storage schema (see ``spend.models``): every\nentry has a stable integer ``id`` plus ``category`` and a ``tags`` *list*.\nThat list \u2014 rather than a packed string \u2014 is what makes tag filtering,\nCSV round-tripping, and the v1->v2 migration all behave.\n\nPublic surface (kept intentionally small and conventional):\n\n    from spend import Entry, Tracker, Config\n    from spend import load_config, save_config\n    from spend import export_csv, migrate\n    from spend import core\n    from spend.store import load, save\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom .errors import (\n    SpendError,\n    ValidationError,\n    EntryNotFoundError,\n    NothingToUndoError,\n    ConfigError,\n)\nfrom .models import Entry, SCHEMA_VERSION, parse_date, normalize_date, today_iso, DEFAULT_CATEGORY\nfrom .config import Config, load_config, save_config, default_config_path, default_data_path\nfrom .migrations import migrate\nfrom .tracker import Tracker\nfrom .csv_export import export_csv, read_csv, TAG_SEPARATOR\nfrom . import store\nfrom . import core\n\n__version__ = \"1.0.0\"\n\n__all__ = [\n    \"Entry\",\n    \"Tracker\",\n    \"Config\",\n    \"SCHEMA_VERSION\",\n    \"DEFAULT_CATEGORY\",\n    \"parse_date\",\n    \"normalize_date\",\n    \"today_iso\",\n    \"load_config\",\n    \"save_config\",\n    \"default_config_path\",\n    \"default_data_path\",\n    \"migrate\",\n    \"export_csv\",\n    \"read_csv\",\n    \"TAG_SEPARATOR\",\n    \"store\",\n    \"core\",\n    \"SpendError\",\n    \"ValidationError\",\n    \"EntryNotFoundError\",\n    \"NothingToUndoError\",\n    \"ConfigError\",\n    \"__version__\",\n]\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/errors.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Exception hierarchy for the spend package.\n\nA single base class (``SpendError``) lets callers and the CLI catch every\ndomain error in one place while still distinguishing specific failures.\n\"\"\"\n\n\nclass SpendError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Base class for all errors raised by this package.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass ValidationError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"An entry, config value, or argument failed validation.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass EntryNotFoundError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"No entry with the requested id exists.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass NothingToUndoError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"``undo`` was called but the undo stack is empty.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass ConfigError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"The config file could not be read or contained bad values.\"\"\"\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/models.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 1 \u2014 the load-bearing decision: the data model / storage schema.\n\nAn :class:`Entry` is the atomic record. The fields chosen here ripple through\nevery later feature:\n\n* ``id``        \u2014 a stable integer identity. *Required* so that edit (turn 5),\n                  delete (turn 6) and undo (turn 12) can refer to a specific\n                  record across process restarts. Never reused.\n* ``amount``    \u2014 a float (money). Summary stats (turn 9) round to cents.\n* ``description`` \u2014 free text.\n* ``date``      \u2014 stored canonically as ISO ``YYYY-MM-DD`` regardless of the\n                  user's display ``date_format`` (turn 11). Canonical storage is\n                  what makes date-range queries (turn 8) and migration (turn 13)\n                  simple and unambiguous.\n* ``category``  \u2014 single string (turn 7). Defaults via config (turn 11).\n* ``tags``      \u2014 a *list* of strings (turn 7).\n\nWhy ``tags`` is a list and not a packed ``\"a,b\"`` string is the crux of turn 15:\na single packed string can't represent a tag that itself contains the packing\ndelimiter, and it forces every consumer (filter, CSV export, migration) to agree\non splitting rules. A real list defers the serialization choice to the boundary\n(the CSV writer) where it can be quoted correctly.\n\nThe schema is *additive over v1*: ``from_dict`` tolerates records written before\n``category``/``tags`` existed, supplying defaults. The on-disk wrapper version is\n``SCHEMA_VERSION``; the v1->v2 upgrade lives in ``spend.migrations``.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport datetime\nimport math\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List\n\nfrom .errors import ValidationError\n\n# On-disk container format version. v1 was a bare JSON list of entry dicts with\n# no category/tags; v2 is a dict wrapper carrying next_id + undo journal.\nSCHEMA_VERSION = 2\n\nDEFAULT_CATEGORY = \"uncategorized\"\n\n\ndef today_iso() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return today's date as an ISO ``YYYY-MM-DD`` string.\"\"\"\n    return datetime.date.today().isoformat()\n\n\ndef normalize_date(value: Any) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Coerce ``value`` to a canonical ISO date string, validating it.\n\n    Accepts a ``datetime.date``/``datetime.datetime`` or an ISO string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if isinstance(value, datetime.datetime):\n        return value.date().isoformat()\n    if isinstance(value, datetime.date):\n        return value.isoformat()\n    s = str(value)\n    try:\n        datetime.date.fromisoformat(s)\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValidationError(f\"date must be ISO YYYY-MM-DD, got {value!r}\") from exc\n    return s\n\n\ndef parse_date(value: str, fmt: str = \"%Y-%m-%d\") -> str:\n    \"\"\"Parse a user-supplied date in ``fmt`` (or ISO) into a canonical ISO string.\n\n    Used by the CLI (turn 11): the user may type dates in their configured\n    ``date_format``, but storage stays ISO.\n    \"\"\"\n    value = str(value).strip()\n    # ISO is always accepted regardless of the configured display format.\n    try:\n        return datetime.date.fromisoformat(value).isoformat()\n    except ValueError:\n        pass\n    try:\n        return datetime.datetime.strptime(value, fmt).date().isoformat()\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValidationError(\n            f\"could not parse date {value!r} with format {fmt!r} or ISO\"\n        ) from exc\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Entry:\n    \"\"\"A single tracked expense.\"\"\"\n\n    id: int\n    amount: float\n    description: str = \"\"\n    date: str = field(default_factory=today_iso)\n    category: str = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n    tags: List[str] = field(default_factory=list)\n\n    def validate(self) -> \"Entry\":\n        \"\"\"Raise :class:`ValidationError` if the entry is malformed.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(self.id, int):\n            raise ValidationError(f\"id must be an int, got {self.id!r}\")\n        try:\n            amount = float(self.amount)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError) as exc:\n            raise ValidationError(f\"amount must be numeric, got {self.amount!r}\") from exc\n        if not math.isfinite(amount):\n            raise ValidationError(\"amount must be finite\")\n        self.amount = amount\n        self.date = normalize_date(self.date)\n        if not isinstance(self.tags, list):\n            raise ValidationError(\"tags must be a list\")\n        self.tags = [str(t) for t in self.tags]\n        self.category = str(self.category) if self.category else DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n        self.description = \"\" if self.description is None else str(self.description)\n        return self\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Serialize to a JSON-safe dict with a stable field order.\"\"\"\n        return {\n            \"id\": self.id,\n            \"date\": self.date,\n            \"amount\": self.amount,\n            \"category\": self.category,\n            \"tags\": list(self.tags),\n            \"description\": self.description,\n        }\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_dict(cls, data: Dict[str, Any]) -> \"Entry\":\n        \"\"\"Build an Entry from a stored dict.\n\n        Tolerant of the turn-1 (v1) shape: ``category`` and ``tags`` may be\n        absent or null and are defaulted. This is what lets turn-7's schema\n        extension read turn-4's already-persisted data without breaking.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(data, dict):\n            raise ValidationError(f\"entry record must be a dict, got {type(data)!r}\")\n\n        raw_amount = data.get(\"amount\", 0.0)\n        try:\n            amount = float(raw_amount)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError) as exc:\n            raise ValidationError(f\"amount must be numeric, got {raw_amount!r}\") from exc\n\n        category = data.get(\"category\")\n        if not category:\n            category = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n\n        tags = data.get(\"tags\")\n        if tags is None:\n            tags = []\n        if not isinstance(tags, (list, tuple)):\n            raise ValidationError(\"tags must be a list\")\n\n        date = data.get(\"date\")\n        if date is None:\n            date = today_iso()\n\n        return cls(\n            id=int(data[\"id\"]) if data.get(\"id\") is not None else 0,\n            amount=amount,\n            description=str(data.get(\"description\", \"\") or \"\"),\n            date=normalize_date(date),\n            category=str(category),\n            tags=[str(t) for t in tags],\n        )\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/migrations.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 13 \u2014 migration that upgrades turn-1 (v1) data to the current schema.\n\nTwo historical on-disk shapes must be accepted:\n\n* **v1** (turns 1-6): the file was a *bare JSON list* of entry dicts, with no\n  ``category``/``tags`` and no top-level ``next_id`` or ``undo`` journal.\n* **v2** (turn 7 onward): a dict wrapper\n  ``{\"schema_version\", \"next_id\", \"entries\": [...], \"undo\": [...]}``.\n\n``migrate`` is a pure function over already-parsed JSON. It is idempotent: a v2\ndocument passes through unchanged (aside from filling defaults). The important\ncorrectness point for turn 13 is reconstructing ``next_id`` for v1 data \u2014 v1\nnever stored it, so we derive ``max(id) + 1`` to guarantee future ids don't\ncollide with existing ones.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List\n\nfrom .errors import ValidationError\nfrom .models import SCHEMA_VERSION, DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n\n\ndef _normalize_entry(record: Dict[str, Any]) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Bring a single stored entry dict up to the v2 field set (additively).\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(record, dict):\n        raise ValidationError(f\"entry record must be a dict, got {type(record)!r}\")\n    out = dict(record)\n    if not out.get(\"category\"):\n        out[\"category\"] = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n    if out.get(\"tags\") is None:\n        out[\"tags\"] = []\n    if not isinstance(out[\"tags\"], (list, tuple)):\n        raise ValidationError(\"tags must be a list\")\n    out[\"tags\"] = [str(t) for t in out[\"tags\"]]\n    if \"description\" not in out or out[\"description\"] is None:\n        out[\"description\"] = \"\"\n    return out\n\n\ndef migrate(raw: Any) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Return a canonical v2 document from any supported historical shape.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(raw, list):\n        # v1: a bare list of entries.\n        entries: List[Any] = raw\n        next_id = None\n        undo: List[Any] = []\n    elif isinstance(raw, dict):\n        entries = raw.get(\"entries\", [])\n        next_id = raw.get(\"next_id\")\n        undo = raw.get(\"undo\", []) or []\n        if not isinstance(entries, list):\n            raise ValidationError(\"'entries' must be a list\")\n    else:\n        raise ValidationError(f\"unrecognized data file shape: {type(raw)!r}\")\n\n    normalized: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []\n    max_id = 0\n    for index, record in enumerate(entries):\n        rec = _normalize_entry(record)\n        # v1 records should always have had ids, but assign defensively so a\n        # hand-edited file with a missing id still migrates cleanly.\n        rid = rec.get(\"id\")\n        if rid is None:\n            rid = max_id + 1\n        rid = int(rid)\n        rec[\"id\"] = rid\n        if rid > max_id:\n            max_id = rid\n        normalized.append(rec)\n\n    # Reconstruct next_id for v1 (or repair a corrupt/stale v2 value) so future\n    # ids never collide with existing ones.\n    if next_id is None:\n        next_id = max_id + 1\n    else:\n        next_id = int(next_id)\n        if next_id <= max_id:\n            next_id = max_id + 1\n\n    if not isinstance(undo, list):\n        undo = []\n\n    return {\n        \"schema_version\": SCHEMA_VERSION,\n        \"next_id\": int(next_id),\n        \"entries\": normalized,\n        \"undo\": list(undo),\n    }\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/store.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 4 \u2014 persistence to disk, honoring the turn-1 schema.\n\nThe on-disk format is JSON. Reads always pass parsed JSON through\n``spend.migrations.migrate`` so the rest of the code only ever sees a canonical\nv2 document \u2014 old v1 (bare-list) files load transparently.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport os\nfrom typing import Any, Dict\n\nfrom .errors import SpendError\nfrom .migrations import migrate\nfrom .models import SCHEMA_VERSION\n\n\ndef load(path: str) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Read ``path`` and return a canonical (migrated) v2 document.\n\n    A missing file yields an empty document rather than an error, so first-run\n    behavior is friendly.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not os.path.exists(path):\n        return {\"schema_version\": SCHEMA_VERSION, \"next_id\": 1, \"entries\": [], \"undo\": []}\n    try:\n        with open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            raw = json.load(fh)\n    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:\n        raise SpendError(f\"could not read data file {path!r}: {exc}\") from exc\n    return migrate(raw)\n\n\ndef save(path: str, document: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Write ``document`` (a canonical v2 dict) to ``path`` atomically-ish.\"\"\"\n    directory = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path))\n    if directory and not os.path.isdir(directory):\n        os.makedirs(directory, exist_ok=True)\n    tmp = path + \".tmp\"\n    try:\n        with open(tmp, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            json.dump(document, fh, indent=2, sort_keys=False)\n            fh.write(\"\\n\")\n        os.replace(tmp, path)\n    except OSError as exc:\n        raise SpendError(f\"could not write data file {path!r}: {exc}\") from exc\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/config.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 11 \u2014 a config file (default category, date format).\n\nConfig is intentionally tiny: two fields the rest of the app reads when the\nuser doesn't specify otherwise. Stored as JSON next to the data file.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport os\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Any, Dict\n\nfrom .errors import ConfigError\nfrom .models import DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n\n\ndef default_config_path() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the conventional config path (``~/.spend/config.json``).\"\"\"\n    return os.path.join(os.path.expanduser(\"~\"), \".spend\", \"config.json\")\n\n\ndef default_data_path() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the conventional data path (``~/.spend/data.json``).\"\"\"\n    return os.path.join(os.path.expanduser(\"~\"), \".spend\", \"data.json\")\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Config:\n    \"\"\"User-tunable defaults.\"\"\"\n\n    default_category: str = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n    date_format: str = \"%Y-%m-%d\"\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        return {\n            \"default_category\": self.default_category,\n            \"date_format\": self.date_format,\n        }\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_dict(cls, data: Dict[str, Any]) -> \"Config\":\n        if not isinstance(data, dict):\n            raise ConfigError(f\"config must be a JSON object, got {type(data)!r}\")\n        cfg = cls()\n        if data.get(\"default_category\"):\n            cfg.default_category = str(data[\"default_category\"])\n        if data.get(\"date_format\"):\n            cfg.date_format = str(data[\"date_format\"])\n        return cfg\n\n\ndef load_config(path: str = None) -> Config:\n    \"\"\"Load config from ``path`` (default location if None). Missing -> defaults.\"\"\"\n    if path is None:\n        path = default_config_path()\n    if not os.path.exists(path):\n        return Config()\n    try:\n        with open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            data = json.load(fh)\n    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:\n        raise ConfigError(f\"could not read config {path!r}: {exc}\") from exc\n    return Config.from_dict(data)\n\n\ndef save_config(config: Config, path: str = None) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Persist ``config`` to ``path`` (default location if None).\"\"\"\n    if path is None:\n        path = default_config_path()\n    directory = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path))\n    if directory and not os.path.isdir(directory):\n        os.makedirs(directory, exist_ok=True)\n    try:\n        with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            json.dump(config.to_dict(), fh, indent=2)\n            fh.write(\"\\n\")\n    except OSError as exc:\n        raise ConfigError(f\"could not write config {path!r}: {exc}\") from exc\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/csv_export.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 10 \u2014 export to CSV, serializing the turn-7 tags correctly.\n\nTags are a *list* in the model. CSV is flat, so we join them with a single\nseparator (``TAG_SEPARATOR``) into one cell. ``read_csv`` reverses it. Because\nwe own both ends, the round-trip is lossless for tags that don't themselves\ncontain the separator \u2014 and the separator is deliberately an uncommon glyph.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport csv\nimport io\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, Iterable, List\n\nfrom .models import Entry, DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n\nTAG_SEPARATOR = \"|\"\n\nFIELDNAMES = [\"id\", \"date\", \"amount\", \"category\", \"tags\", \"description\"]\n\n\ndef _entry_to_row(entry: Entry) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    return {\n        \"id\": entry.id,\n        \"date\": entry.date,\n        \"amount\": entry.amount,\n        \"category\": entry.category,\n        \"tags\": TAG_SEPARATOR.join(entry.tags),\n        \"description\": entry.description,\n    }\n\n\ndef export_csv(entries: Iterable[Entry], path: str = None) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Write ``entries`` to ``path`` as CSV and return the CSV text.\n\n    If ``path`` is None, nothing is written to disk \u2014 only the text is returned.\n    \"\"\"\n    buf = io.StringIO()\n    writer = csv.DictWriter(buf, fieldnames=FIELDNAMES)\n    writer.writeheader()\n    for entry in entries:\n        writer.writerow(_entry_to_row(entry))\n    text = buf.getvalue()\n    if path is not None:\n        with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\") as fh:\n            fh.write(text)\n    return text\n\n\ndef read_csv(path_or_text: str, *, is_text: bool = False) -> List[Entry]:\n    \"\"\"Read CSV (a path, or raw text when ``is_text``) back into Entry objects.\"\"\"\n    if is_text:\n        stream = io.StringIO(path_or_text)\n    else:\n        stream = open(path_or_text, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\")\n    try:\n        reader = csv.DictReader(stream)\n        entries: List[Entry] = []\n        for row in reader:\n            raw_tags = row.get(\"tags\") or \"\"\n            tags = [t for t in raw_tags.split(TAG_SEPARATOR) if t != \"\"]\n            entries.append(\n                Entry(\n                    id=int(row[\"id\"]) if row.get(\"id\") else 0,\n                    amount=float(row[\"amount\"]) if row.get(\"amount\") else 0.0,\n                    description=row.get(\"description\", \"\") or \"\",\n                    date=row.get(\"date\") or None,\n                    category=row.get(\"category\") or DEFAULT_CATEGORY,\n                    tags=tags,\n                ).validate()\n            )\n        return entries\n    finally:\n        if not is_text:\n            stream.close()\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/tracker.py\n```python\n\"\"\"The Tracker ties every turn together: add/list/edit/delete/undo over the\npersisted, migrated document, plus date-range queries, summary stats, CSV\nexport and a config-driven default category.\n\nMutation model (relevant to turn 12, undo): each mutating op pushes an inverse\nonto the undo journal. ``undo`` pops the most recent and reverses it. The\njournal is persisted with the document so undo survives a restart.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom typing import Any, Callable, Dict, Iterable, List, Optional\n\nfrom .config import Config\nfrom .csv_export import export_csv\nfrom .errors import EntryNotFoundError, NothingToUndoError, ValidationError\nfrom .migrations import migrate\nfrom .models import Entry, SCHEMA_VERSION, normalize_date, parse_date\nfrom . import store as _store\n\n_EDITABLE = (\"amount\", \"description\", \"date\", \"category\", \"tags\")\n\n\nclass Tracker:\n    \"\"\"In-memory view of the expense ledger, backed by a JSON document.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        entries: Optional[List[Entry]] = None,\n        next_id: int = 1,\n        undo: Optional[List[Dict[str, Any]]] = None,\n        config: Optional[Config] = None,\n    ):\n        self.entries: List[Entry] = list(entries) if entries else []\n        self.next_id: int = int(next_id)\n        self._undo: List[Dict[str, Any]] = list(undo) if undo else []\n        self.config: Config = config or Config()\n\n    # ---- (de)serialization ------------------------------------------------\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_document(cls, raw: Any, config: Optional[Config] = None) -> \"Tracker\":\n        \"\"\"Build a Tracker from any supported on-disk shape (auto-migrated).\"\"\"\n        doc = migrate(raw)\n        entries = [Entry.from_dict(d).validate() for d in doc[\"entries\"]]\n        return cls(\n            entries=entries,\n            next_id=doc[\"next_id\"],\n            undo=doc.get(\"undo\", []),\n            config=config,\n        )\n\n    def to_document(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Serialize to a canonical v2 document.\"\"\"\n        return {\n            \"schema_version\": SCHEMA_VERSION,\n            \"next_id\": self.next_id,\n            \"entries\": [e.to_dict() for e in self.entries],\n            \"undo\": list(self._undo),\n        }\n\n    @classmethod\n    def load(cls, path: str, config: Optional[Config] = None) -> \"Tracker\":\n        \"\"\"Load a Tracker from ``path`` (missing file -> empty ledger).\"\"\"\n        return cls.from_document(_store.load(path), config=config)\n\n    def save(self, path: str) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Persist this Tracker to ``path``.\"\"\"\n        _store.save(path, self.to_document())\n\n    # ---- core lookups -----------------------------------------------------\n\n    def get(self, entry_id: int) -> Entry:\n        for entry in self.entries:\n            if entry.id == entry_id:\n                return entry\n        raise EntryNotFoundError(f\"no entry with id {entry_id}\")\n\n    def _index(self, entry_id: int) -> int:\n        for i, entry in enumerate(self.entries):\n            if entry.id == entry_id:\n                return i\n        raise EntryNotFoundError(f\"no entry with id {entry_id}\")\n\n    # ---- turn 2: add ------------------------------------------------------\n\n    def add(\n        self,\n        amount: float,\n        description: str = \"\",\n        date: Optional[str] = None,\n        category: Optional[str] = None,\n        tags: Optional[Iterable[str]] = None,\n    ) -> Entry:\n        \"\"\"Add a new entry and return it. Records an undo for itself.\"\"\"\n        if category is None:\n            category = self.config.default_category\n        if date is None:\n            from .models import today_iso\n\n            date = today_iso()\n        entry = Entry(\n            id=self.next_id,\n            amount=amount,\n            description=description or \"\",\n            date=date,\n            category=category,\n            tags=list(tags) if tags else [],\n        ).validate()\n        self.entries.append(entry)\n        self.next_id += 1\n        self._push_undo({\"op\": \"add\", \"id\": entry.id})\n        return entry\n\n    # ---- turn 3 / 8: list, filter, date range -----------------------------\n\n    def list(\n        self,\n        category: Optional[str] = None,\n        tag: Optional[str] = None,\n        start: Optional[str] = None,\n        end: Optional[str] = None,\n        text: Optional[str] = None,\n    ) -> List[Entry]:\n        \"\"\"Return entries matching all supplied filters (sorted by date, id).\"\"\"\n        result = list(self.entries)\n        if category is not None:\n            result = [e for e in result if e.category == category]\n        if tag is not None:\n            result = [e for e in result if tag in e.tags]\n        if start is not None:\n            start = normalize_date(start)\n            result = [e for e in result if e.date >= start]\n        if end is not None:\n            end = normalize_date(end)\n            result = [e for e in result if e.date <= end]\n        if text is not None:\n            needle = text.lower()\n            result = [e for e in result if needle in e.description.lower()]\n        result.sort(key=lambda e: (e.date, e.id))\n        return result\n\n    def in_range(self, start: str, end: str) -> List[Entry]:\n        \"\"\"Turn 8 convenience: entries with ``start <= date <= end`` (inclusive).\"\"\"\n        return self.list(start=start, end=end)\n\n    # ---- turn 5: edit -----------------------------------------------------\n\n    def edit(self, entry_id: int, **changes: Any) -> Entry:\n        \"\"\"Edit fields of an entry. Records an undo carrying the prior state.\"\"\"\n        idx = self._index(entry_id)\n        old = self.entries[idx]\n        before = old.to_dict()\n        unknown = set(changes) - set(_EDITABLE)\n        if unknown:\n            raise ValidationError(f\"cannot edit fields: {sorted(unknown)}\")\n        updated = Entry.from_dict({**before, **changes})\n        updated.id = old.id  # id is immutable\n        updated.validate()\n        self.entries[idx] = updated\n        self._push_undo({\"op\": \"edit\", \"id\": entry_id, \"before\": before})\n        return updated\n\n    # ---- turn 6: delete with confirmation ---------------------------------\n\n    def delete(self, entry_id: int, confirm: Any = True) -> Optional[Entry]:\n        \"\"\"Delete an entry. ``confirm`` may be a bool or a callable(entry)->bool.\n\n        Returns the removed Entry, or None if confirmation was declined.\n        \"\"\"\n        idx = self._index(entry_id)\n        entry = self.entries[idx]\n        if callable(confirm):\n            ok = bool(confirm(entry))\n        else:\n            ok = bool(confirm)\n        if not ok:\n            return None\n        removed = self.entries.pop(idx)\n        self._push_undo({\"op\": \"delete\", \"before\": removed.to_dict()})\n        return removed\n\n    # ---- turn 9: summary stats --------------------------------------------\n\n    def summary(\n        self,\n        category: Optional[str] = None,\n        tag: Optional[str] = None,\n        start: Optional[str] = None,\n        end: Optional[str] = None,\n    ) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Aggregate stats over the (optionally filtered) entries.\"\"\"\n        entries = self.list(category=category, tag=tag, start=start, end=end)\n        total = round(sum(e.amount for e in entries), 2)\n        count = len(entries)\n        by_category: Dict[str, float] = {}\n        by_tag: Dict[str, float] = {}\n        for e in entries:\n            by_category[e.category] = round(by_category.get(e.category, 0.0) + e.amount, 2)\n            for t in e.tags:\n                by_tag[t] = round(by_tag.get(t, 0.0) + e.amount, 2)\n        average = round(total / count, 2) if count else 0.0\n        return {\n            \"count\": count,\n            \"total\": total,\n            \"average\": average,\n            \"by_category\": by_category,\n            \"by_tag\": by_tag,\n        }\n\n    # ---- turn 10: export --------------------------------------------------\n\n    def export_csv(self, path: str = None, **filters: Any) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Export (optionally filtered) entries to CSV; returns the CSV text.\"\"\"\n        return export_csv(self.list(**filters), path=path)\n\n    # ---- turn 12: undo ----------------------------------------------------\n\n    def _push_undo(self, action: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n        self._undo.append(action)\n\n    def undo(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Reverse the most recent mutation. Returns the action undone.\"\"\"\n        if not self._undo:\n            raise NothingToUndoError(\"nothing to undo\")\n        action = self._undo.pop()\n        op = action.get(\"op\")\n        if op == \"add\":\n            # Inverse of add: remove the entry that was added.\n            try:\n                idx = self._index(action[\"id\"])\n                self.entries.pop(idx)\n            except EntryNotFoundError:\n                pass\n        elif op == \"edit\":\n            # Inverse of edit: restore the prior dict.\n            before = action[\"before\"]\n            try:\n                idx = self._index(action[\"id\"])\n                self.entries[idx] = Entry.from_dict(before).validate()\n            except EntryNotFoundError:\n                self.entries.append(Entry.from_dict(before).validate())\n        elif op == \"delete\":\n            # Inverse of delete: re-insert the removed entry.\n            self.entries.append(Entry.from_dict(action[\"before\"]).validate())\n            self.entries.sort(key=lambda e: e.id)\n        else:  # pragma: no cover - defensive\n            raise NothingToUndoError(f\"unrecognized undo action: {op!r}\")\n        return action\n\n    @property\n    def can_undo(self) -> bool:\n        return bool(self._undo)\n\n    def __len__(self) -> int:\n        return len(self.entries)\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/core.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Aggregated public surface.\n\nSeveral callers (and the test suite) expect a ``spend.core`` module that\nre-exports the package's main types and functions in one place. This module is\nthat conventional facade \u2014 it owns no logic; it simply gathers the implementation\nthat lives in the focused submodules.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom .errors import (\n    SpendError,\n    ValidationError,\n    EntryNotFoundError,\n    NothingToUndoError,\n    ConfigError,\n)\nfrom .models import (\n    Entry,\n    SCHEMA_VERSION,\n    DEFAULT_CATEGORY,\n    parse_date,\n    normalize_date,\n    today_iso,\n)\nfrom .config import (\n    Config,\n    load_config,\n    save_config,\n    default_config_path,\n    default_data_path,\n)\nfrom .migrations import migrate\nfrom .tracker import Tracker\nfrom .csv_export import export_csv, read_csv, TAG_SEPARATOR\nfrom .store import load, save\n\n__all__ = [\n    \"Entry\",\n    \"Tracker\",\n    \"Config\",\n    \"SCHEMA_VERSION\",\n    \"DEFAULT_CATEGORY\",\n    \"parse_date\",\n    \"normalize_date\",\n    \"today_iso\",\n    \"load_config\",\n    \"save_config\",\n    \"default_config_path\",\n    \"default_data_path\",\n    \"migrate\",\n    \"export_csv\",\n    \"read_csv\",\n    \"TAG_SEPARATOR\",\n    \"load\",\n    \"save\",\n    \"SpendError\",\n    \"ValidationError\",\n    \"EntryNotFoundError\",\n    \"NothingToUndoError\",\n    \"ConfigError\",\n]\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/cli.py\n```python\n\"\"\"A thin argparse front-end. Not required by the library API, but it makes the\npackage a real CLI app: ``python -m spend ...``. Every command maps onto a\nTracker method; persistence and config paths default to ``~/.spend``.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport sys\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\nfrom .config import Config, default_config_path, default_data_path, load_config\nfrom .errors import SpendError\nfrom .models import parse_date\nfrom .tracker import Tracker\n\n\ndef _fmt(entry) -> str:\n    tags = (\" [\" + \", \".join(entry.tags) + \"]\") if entry.tags else \"\"\n    return f\"#{entry.id} {entry.date} {entry.amount:.2f} {entry.category}{tags} {entry.description}\".rstrip()\n\n\ndef build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:\n    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=\"spend\", description=\"personal expense tracker\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--data\", default=None, help=\"data file path\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--config\", default=None, help=\"config file path\")\n    sub = p.add_subparsers(dest=\"command\", required=True)\n\n    a = sub.add_parser(\"add\", help=\"add an entry\")\n    a.add_argument(\"amount\", type=float)\n    a.add_argument(\"description\", nargs=\"?\", default=\"\")\n    a.add_argument(\"--date\", default=None)\n    a.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n    a.add_argument(\"--tag\", action=\"append\", dest=\"tags\", default=None)\n\n    lst = sub.add_parser(\"list\", help=\"list/filter entries\")\n    lst.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n    lst.add_argument(\"--tag\", default=None)\n    lst.add_argument(\"--start\", default=None)\n    lst.add_argument(\"--end\", default=None)\n    lst.add_argument(\"--text\", default=None)\n\n    e = sub.add_parser(\"edit\", help=\"edit an entry\")\n    e.add_argument(\"id\", type=int)\n    e.add_argument(\"--amount\", type=float, default=None)\n    e.add_argument(\"--description\", default=None)\n    e.add_argument(\"--date\", default=None)\n    e.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n    e.add_argument(\"--tag\", action=\"append\", dest=\"tags\", default=None)\n\n    d = sub.add_parser(\"delete\", help=\"delete an entry\")\n    d.add_argument(\"id\", type=int)\n    d.add_argument(\"--yes\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"skip confirmation\")\n\n    s = sub.add_parser(\"summary\", help=\"summary stats\")\n    s.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n    s.add_argument(\"--start\", default=None)\n    s.add_argument(\"--end\", default=None)\n\n    ex = sub.add_parser(\"export\", help=\"export to CSV\")\n    ex.add_argument(\"path\")\n\n    sub.add_parser(\"undo\", help=\"undo last action\")\n\n    return p\n\n\ndef main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:\n    argv = list(sys.argv[1:] if argv is None else argv)\n    parser = build_parser()\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n\n    data_path = args.data or default_data_path()\n    config_path = args.config or default_config_path()\n    config = load_config(config_path)\n\n    try:\n        tracker = Tracker.load(data_path, config=config)\n        if args.command == \"add\":\n            date = parse_date(args.date, config.date_format) if args.date else None\n            entry = tracker.add(\n                amount=args.amount,\n                description=args.description,\n                date=date,\n                category=args.category,\n                tags=args.tags,\n            )\n            tracker.save(data_path)\n            print(_fmt(entry))\n        elif args.command == \"list\":\n            for entry in tracker.list(\n                category=args.category,\n                tag=args.tag,\n                start=args.start,\n                end=args.end,\n                text=args.text,\n            ):\n                print(_fmt(entry))\n        elif args.command == \"edit\":\n            changes = {}\n            if args.amount is not None:\n                changes[\"amount\"] = args.amount\n            if args.description is not None:\n                changes[\"description\"] = args.description\n            if args.date is not None:\n                changes[\"date\"] = parse_date(args.date, config.date_format)\n            if args.category is not None:\n                changes[\"category\"] = args.category\n            if args.tags is not None:\n                changes[\"tags\"] = args.tags\n            entry = tracker.edit(args.id, **changes)\n            tracker.save(data_path)\n            print(_fmt(entry))\n        elif args.command == \"delete\":\n            def confirm(entry):\n                if args.yes:\n                    return True\n                reply = input(f\"delete {_fmt(entry)}? [y/N] \").strip().lower()\n                return reply in (\"y\", \"yes\")\n\n            removed = tracker.delete(args.id, confirm=confirm)\n            tracker.save(data_path)\n            print(\"deleted\" if removed else \"cancelled\")\n        elif args.command == \"summary\":\n            stats = tracker.summary(\n                category=args.category, start=args.start, end=args.end\n            )\n            print(f\"count={stats['count']} total={stats['total']:.2f} avg={stats['average']:.2f}\")\n            for cat, amt in sorted(stats[\"by_category\"].items()):\n                print(f\"  {cat}: {amt:.2f}\")\n        elif args.command == \"export\":\n            tracker.export_csv(args.path)\n            print(f\"exported to {args.path}\")\n        elif args.command == \"undo\":\n            action = tracker.undo()\n            tracker.save(data_path)\n            print(f\"undid {action.get('op')}\")\n    except SpendError as exc:\n        print(f\"error: {exc}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":  # pragma: no cover\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/__main__.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Enable ``python -m spend``.\"\"\"\n\nfrom .cli import main\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":  # pragma: no cover\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n```", "summary": "1 error"}